    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p13-q11-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Prove that every odd integer is the difference of two consecutive squares. For example, $7=4^2-3^2$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$(n+1)^2-n^2=2n+1$ for every $n\\in\\mathbb Z$.",
          "worked": "Let the odd integer be $2n+1$, where $n\\in\\mathbb Z$. Then\n$$\\begin{align*}(n+1)^2-n^2&=n^2+2n+1-n^2\\\\(n+1)^2-n^2&=2n+1.\\end{align*}$$\nThus every odd integer is the difference of the squares of the consecutive integers $n$ and $n+1$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 44
      },
      {
        "id": "p13-q11-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Hence, prove that every odd integer greater than 1 is realised as the side of some right-angled triangle with integer side lengths.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "For $k=2a+1>1$, the required integer side lengths are $2a+1$, $2a^2+2a$ and $2a^2+2a+1$.\n\nApply part (a) to the odd integer $k^2$.",
          "worked": "Let $k=2a+1$, where $a$ is a positive integer. Then\n$$\\begin{align*}k^2&=4a^2+4a+1\\\\k^2&=2(2a^2+2a)+1.\\end{align*}$$\nPut $N=2a^2+2a$. By part (a),\n$$\\begin{align*}(N+1)^2-N^2&=2N+1\\\\(N+1)^2-N^2&=k^2.\\end{align*}$$\nHence $k^2+N^2=(N+1)^2$. The positive integers $k$, $N$ and $N+1$ are therefore the side lengths of a right-angled triangle. They are $2a+1$, $2a^2+2a$ and $2a^2+2a+1$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 68
      }
    ]
  },
